To address climate change, global negotiations rely heavily on technical reports, 
yet often struggle to integrate them into discussions due to a lack of relevant 
first-hand experience among delegates. This research presents a system capable 
of bridging this knowledge gap by providing personal connections between an 
individual's skills and the corresponding technical information, facilitating 
meaningful contributions. The system achieves this by leveraging a vast, 
structured database containing climate-related information and expert profiles, 
enabling effective insight sharing through simple input of a person's skills. 
Currently applied in EU-funded climate workshops, users can explore relevant 
concepts, establish relationships with other participants, and enhance their own 
understanding of the subject, as demonstrated through a user-study showing 
significant knowledge gain among participants. This paper outlines the approach, 
evaluates its impact, and identifies opportunities for further improvement. The 
climate knowledge base introduced here holds potential to empower climate 
negotiations and can be adapted to various other collaborative environments. 

